🧠 Introduction

The Java Collections Framework provides different collection classes for different use cases.

The most commonly used implementations are:

✔ ArrayList
✔ LinkedList
✔ HashSet
✔ TreeSet

👉 Choosing the right collection improves application performance and efficiency.

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

🔹 1. ArrayList

`ArrayList` is a dynamic array that grows automatically when elements are added.

---

📌 Features

✔ Dynamic size

✔ Maintains insertion order

✔ Allows duplicate elements

✔ Fast random access using index

---

📌 Example

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;

public class Demo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        ArrayList<String> languages =
                new ArrayList<>();

        languages.add("Java");
        languages.add("Python");
        languages.add("Java");

        System.out.println(languages);
    }
}
```

---

🎯 Advantages

✔ Fast element access
✔ Easy traversal
✔ Dynamic resizing

---

⚠️ Disadvantages

❌ Slow insertion/deletion in the middle

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

🔹 2. LinkedList

`LinkedList` stores elements using nodes connected through links.

---

📌 Features

✔ Dynamic size

✔ Maintains insertion order

✔ Allows duplicate elements

✔ Efficient insertion and deletion

---

📌 Example

```java
import java.util.LinkedList;

public class Demo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        LinkedList<String> cities =
                new LinkedList<>();

        cities.add("Delhi");
        cities.add("Mumbai");
        cities.addFirst("Kolkata");

        System.out.println(cities);
    }
}
```

---

🎯 Advantages

✔ Fast insertion
✔ Fast deletion
✔ Can be used as Queue and Deque

---

⚠️ Disadvantages

❌ Slower random access compared to ArrayList

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

🔹 3. HashSet

`HashSet` stores only unique elements.

---

📌 Features

✔ No duplicate elements

✔ Unordered collection

✔ Fast searching

---

📌 Example

```java
import java.util.HashSet;

public class Demo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        HashSet<Integer> numbers =
                new HashSet<>();

        numbers.add(10);
        numbers.add(20);
        numbers.add(10);

        System.out.println(numbers);
    }
}
```

---

📌 Output

```text
[10, 20]
```

---

🎯 Advantages

✔ Very fast search
✔ Prevents duplicates

---

⚠️ Disadvantages

❌ Does not maintain insertion order

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

🔹 4. TreeSet

`TreeSet` stores unique elements in sorted order.

---

📌 Features

✔ Automatically sorts elements
✔ No duplicates
✔ Uses Red-Black Tree internally

---

📌 Example

```java
import java.util.TreeSet;

public class Demo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        TreeSet<Integer> numbers =
                new TreeSet<>();

        numbers.add(40);
        numbers.add(10);
        numbers.add(30);
        numbers.add(20);

        System.out.println(numbers);
    }
}
```

---

📌 Output

```text
[10, 20, 30, 40]
```

---

🎯 Advantages

✔ Sorted data

✔ Efficient searching

---

⚠️ Disadvantages

❌ Slower than HashSet

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

📊 Comparison Table

| Feature            | ArrayList     | LinkedList         | HashSet    | TreeSet        |
| ------------------ | ------------- | ------------------ | ---------- | -------------- |
| Maintains Order    | ✔ Yes         | ✔ Yes             | ❌ No      | ✔ Sorted      |
| Allows Duplicates  | ✔ Yes         | ✔ Yes             | ❌ No      | ❌ No         |
| Index Access       | ✔ Fast        | ❌ Slow           | ❌ No      | ❌ No         |
| Insertion          | Medium        | Fast               | Fast       | Medium         |
| Searching          | Medium        | Medium             | Fast       | Fast           |
| Internal Structure | Dynamic Array | Doubly Linked List | Hash Table | Red-Black Tree |

